      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the inverse of the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we need to understand the properties of such a projection matrix and its inverse.\n\n1. **Understanding Projection Matrix and Its Inverse**:\n   The projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) for projecting onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{a} \\mathbf{a}^T}{\\mathbf{a}^T \\mathbf{a}}\n   \\]\n   This matrix maps any vector \\(\\mathbf{x}\\) to the projection of \\(\\mathbf{x}\\) onto \\(\\mathbf{a}\\).\n\n2. **Special Case of Inverse of Projection Matrix**:\n   A projection matrix onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} a_1 \\\\ a_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\) has an inverse if and only if it is not deficient (does not have a zero eigenvalue). For a general vector, the projection matrix is not invertible because its eigenspace (the span of \\(\\mathbf{a}\\)) has dimension 1, making the matrix singular.\n\n3. **Specific Case Analysis**:\n   For the vector \\(\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{pmatrix} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -3 \\end{pmatrix}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determinant Analysis**:\n   The determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{det}(\\mathbf{P}) = \\left( \\frac{1}{13} \\right)^2 (3^2 + 2^2) = \\frac{1}{169} (9 + 4) = \\frac{13 \\cdot 13}{169} = \\frac{13^2}{169} = \\frac{1}{169} \\cdot 13^2\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\mathbf{a}\\) is an eigenvector of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) corresponding to the eigenvalue \\(\\lambda = 0\\), \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is not invertible (singular). Therefore, \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist and should be the zero matrix.\n\nConclusion:\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
