      "text": "Given the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, let $\\mathbf{a} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$.\n\nThe normal vector $\\mathbf{d}$ to $\\mathbf{a}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{a}$ and is perpendicular to the line that projects $\\mathbf{a}$ onto itself. We can find $\\mathbf{d}$ by using the cross product:\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{d} &= \\mathbf{a} \\times \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{vmatrix} \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\ -3 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\end{vmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} (-2)(0) - (0)(0) \\\\ (0)(0) - (-3)(0) \\\\ (-3)(0) - (-2)(0) \\end{pmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\end{align*}\n\nThus, $\\mathbf{d} = \\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}$, and because the span of $\\mathbf{d}$ is empty, $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\mathbf{d}$ are collinear.\n$\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0 \\end{pmatrix}}$",
