      "text": "To find the matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) for projecting onto the vector \\(\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), we start by recalling the formula for the projection matrix onto a vector \\(\\mathbf{v}\\). If \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} v_1 \\\\ v_2 \\end{pmatrix}\\), the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is given by\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{v} \\mathbf{v}^T}{\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v}}.\n\\]\n\nHere, \\(\\mathbf{v} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\). First, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{v} \\mathbf{v}^T\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v} \\mathbf{v}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nNext, we calculate \\(\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{v}^T \\mathbf{v} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13.\n\\]\n\nThus, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nTo find the inverse of this matrix, we use the formula for the inverse of a 2x2 matrix \\(\\begin{pmatrix} a & b \\\\ c & d \\end{pmatrix}\\), which is \\(\\frac{1}{ad - bc} \\begin{pmatrix} d & -b \\\\ -c & a \\end{pmatrix}\\). For our matrix \\(\\mathbf{P} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\\), we have \\(a = \\frac{9}{13}\\), \\(b = \\frac{6}{13}\\), \\(c = \\frac{6}{13}\\), and \\(d = \\frac{4}{13}\\). The determinant \\(ad - bc\\) is\n\n\\[\nad - bc = \\left(\\frac{9}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{4}{13}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{6}{13}\\right)\\left(\\frac{6}{13}\\right) = \\frac{36}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = 0.\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant is zero, the inverse \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist. Therefore, the answer is the zero matrix. We box the final answer as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
